      "text": "Given:\n\\[\n\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\n\\]\n\nWe need to find:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\n\\]\n\nWe start by using the property of determinants that states:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{B}) = \\det \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\det \\mathbf{B}\n\\]\n\nFirst, consider the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\):\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\n\\]\nWe can express \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) as \\(\\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = \\det (\\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M} \\cdot \\mathbf{M})\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of determinants again, we get:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = \\det (\\mathbf{M}) \\cdot \\det (\\mathbf{M}) \\cdot \\det (\\mathbf{M}) \\cdot \\det (\\mathbf{M})\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we substitute this value into the expression:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2)\n\\]\n\nNow we calculate the product:\n\\[\n(-2) \\cdot (-2) = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\cdot (-2) = -8\n\\]\n\\[\n-8 \\cdot (-2) = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
